Levels are visually designed to be inspired by real-life structures in some way but not directly applicable in a real-world setting.

At the beginning of a level, the player is required to reach a powerup that will trigger the start of the level. During this period, there is no music and players are encouraged to explore the layout of the level.

Players and enemies will tend towards the bottom of a level. It is easier to travel to the bottom of a level than the upper areas, so the level design needs to compensate.

Enemy types:
- "Fodder" enemies have the following functions:
    - The player needs to have a constant supply of energy, so Fodder types ensure there's always a source of energy.
    - Fodder types should have simple planning and are very predictable. This makes them very simple to deal with one at a time.
    - Fodder types should gradually increase difficulty as more are introduced--each one should introduce a bit more mental planning.
- "Problem: enemies have the following functions:
    - Represent a novel and immediate threat. One or a few Problem types should pose a constant mental load that requires high player prioritization.
    - Should represent a local peak in tension.